  "final_text": "Both objects exist and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-47827205 Web logs overview\"**\nSix panels over the last 7 days, all on the Kibana Sample Data Logs view:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Total bytes transferred (sum of `bytes`)\n- Requests by response code (pie, `response.keyword`)\n- Requests over time by response code (bar, date histogram on `timestamp`, split by response code)\n- Top requested extensions (bar, `extension.keyword`)\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-47827205 5xx spike\"**\n- Type: Elasticsearch query (`.es-query`) on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`\n- Schedule: every 1 minute\n- Condition: count of documents over the last 5 minutes is greater than 10\n- Query: `response.keyword` in \"500\" through \"599\"\n- Status: enabled, currently `ok`\n- Tag: `EFF-47827205`\n- No notification action is attached, so the rule will evaluate and show alerts in Stack Management but won't send anything to a connector.\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- `response` is a text field, so I matched the 5xx codes with a `terms` query on `response.keyword` rather than a numeric range.\n- The dashboard uses a 7-day window. The rule uses a 5-minute window, as you specified.\n\n**Not verified:** I couldn't check the data values directly. `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` isn't set in my shell, so I couldn't run raw queries against the index. I confirmed the field names with `describe_data_view`, and the dashboard and rule are both present in Kibana.",
